      "background_kind": "curated",
      "heading": "1 · Open, then close",
      "sentence": "First invent many possibilities. Then pick what fits the needs of the brief — one creative act can hold both moves.",
      "quote": "The same creative act may involve both divergent and convergent thinking.",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Craft 2000, 30)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/creativity-techniques/u-1-introduction-creativity/#ref-craft-2000"
      },
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "semantic_tags": {
        "themes": [
          "divergent-convergent",
          "brief-craft"
        ],
        "cidoc": [
          "crm:E33_Linguistic_Object"
        ],
        "getty_aat": [
          "creativity (concept)"
        ],
        "dcterms": [
          "dcterms:type=Text",
          "dcterms:subject=divergent-convergent"
        ]
      },
      "image_brief": "Michelangelo, a sheet of studies connected with the Libyan Sibyl, c. 1510–11: a large worked-up leg study beside two small trial sketches of a seated figure — options opened quickly, one carried through in detail.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Studies for the Libyan Sibyl (recto); Studies for the Libyan Sibyl and a small Sketch for a Seated Figure (verso) MET DP807402.jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "U1.masterclass-1",
      "notes": "- First we open: invent many options. That is divergent thinking.\n- Then we close: pick what fits the needs of the brief. That is convergent thinking.\n- One creative job often needs both moves.\nSource: the quote and the open/close distinction (divergent; convergent = possibilities that fit a set of needs) are (Craft 2000, 30). Other bullets are course wording.\nAsk: Think of your last project. Where did you open, and where did you close?"
    },
