      "background_kind": "diagram",
      "heading": "2 · Four skills tests measure",
      "sentence": "Fluency (many ideas), flexibility (many angles), originality (ideas few other people have), elaboration (work the details). Many workshops try to raise these scores.",
      "quote": "Fluency - the ability to produce a large number of ideas or solutions to a problem rapidly.",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Chen 2011, 26)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/creativity-techniques/u-1-introduction-creativity/#ref-chen-2011"
      },
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "semantic_tags": {
        "themes": [
          "fluency",
          "trainable-skills"
        ],
        "cidoc": [
          "crm:E33_Linguistic_Object"
        ],
        "getty_aat": [
          "creativity (concept)"
        ],
        "dcterms": [
          "dcterms:type=Text",
          "dcterms:subject=fluency"
        ]
      },
      "image_brief": "Katsushika Hokusai, a page of the Hokusai Manga sketchbooks (from 1814): many quick, varied figures on one page — fluency (many ideas), flexibility (many kinds) and elaboration made visible.",
      "notes": "- Guilford's four abilities, as summarised by Chen, give us shared words: fluency (many ideas quickly), flexibility (several angles), originality (ideas few other people produce), elaboration (work the details).\n- Many workshops try to raise these scores.\n- A higher score is not the same as a better idea in a real brief. That is the next slide.\nSource: the four abilities and the Fluency quote are (Chen 2011, 26); fluency, flexibility, originality as what \"most workshops try to enhance\" is (Csikszentmihalyi 1996, chap. 3). Other bullets are course wording.\nAsk: Which of the four skills do you use most? Which one do you avoid?"
    },
