      "citation": {
        "label": "(Tao of Creativity)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/creativity-techniques/u-1-introduction-creativity/#tao-of-creativity"
      },
      "image_brief": "Auguste Rodin, The Thinker (modelled 1880–81): a figure halted in thought before any tool is picked up, to picture framing the unclear problem before choosing a technique.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Auguste Rodin - The Thinker - 1917.42 - Cleveland Museum of Art.tif",
      "media_slot_id": "U1.masterclass-4",
      "notes": "- Before you pick a creativity technique, say what is fuzzy in the brief.\n- Tools help after the problem is framed.\n- Problems are not given; they are built from an unclear situation, and how you state one already points to an answer.\nSource: the slide quote is a Tao of Creativity line; the lesson cites (Schön 1983, 40), (Buchanan 1992, 16) and (Csikszentmihalyi 1996, chap. 4) for the third bullet: problems are constructed from uncertain situations (Schön), every formulation of a wicked problem corresponds to a formulation of a solution (Buchanan), presented vs discovered problems (Csikszentmihalyi). Other bullets are course wording.\nAsk: What question does your current brief still refuse to answer?"
